NCAA Tournament Betting Analysis: Key Factors & Frameworks
March 2026 | Based on Bart Torvik advanced metrics and historical ATS data

Overview
This document outlines the most important quantitative and structural factors for predicting spread and total outcomes in the NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament. It is intended for use in training a predictive model and integrates insights from Bart Torvik's tempo-free efficiency system with historical ATS trends.

1. Core Modeling Foundation: Tempo-Free Efficiency
The entire Torvik system is built on points per possession (PPP) rather than raw scoring, eliminating pace as a confounding variable.
Key Metrics (from barttorvik.com)
MetricDescriptionPredictive UseAdjOEAdjusted Offensive Efficiency — points scored per 100 possessions, opponent-adjustedPrimary spread inputAdjDEAdjusted Defensive Efficiency — points allowed per 100 possessions, opponent-adjustedPrimary spread input; higher weight than offenseAdjTempoAdjusted possessions per 40 minutesPrimary totals inputeFG%Effective Field Goal % — accounts for 3pt value, pace-independentShooting environment signalBarthagPower rating; win probability vs. average D1 teamMoneyline / win probability baseWABWins Above Bubble — quality of schedule-adjusted performanceTournament seed vs. efficiency gap signal
Spread Calculation Logic
Expected Score (Team A) = Team A AdjOE × (Team B AdjDE / 100)
Expected Score (Team B) = Team B AdjOE × (Team A AdjDE / 100)
Expected Possessions = Blended tempo of both teams
Raw Spread = (Expected Score A − Expected Score B) scaled to expected possessions
Home Court Adjustment

Apply ±1.4% to each team's AdjOE and AdjDE for home/away
Equivalent to approximately 3.5 points per game
All tournament games are neutral site — no home court adjustment applied


2. Defense Outweighs Offense in March
Finding: Defensive efficiency is a stronger predictor of tournament ATS outcomes than offensive efficiency.

Teams ranked inside the top 30 nationally in AdjDE outperform seed expectations in single-elimination play
Tournament games are played on neutral sites against unfamiliar opponents — offensive systems struggle to replicate regular-season efficiency
Defensive ratings are more stable across venue and opponent contexts
Allowed eFG% (opponent shooting quality suppression) is the single most impactful defensive sub-metric on Barthag

Model implication: Weight AdjDE more heavily than AdjOE when projecting spread outcomes. A suggested ratio based on research is approximately 55% defense / 45% offense in efficiency differential calculations.

3. Seeding vs. Spread: The Core Disconnect
Finding: Seed number has no statistically significant impact on ATS outcomes.

Books partially set lines based on seed; public money reinforces this
The market systematically overvalues higher seeds, inflating spreads beyond what efficiency differentials support
This creates exploitable inefficiencies, particularly for 10–13 seeds with strong defensive profiles

The 5 vs. 12 Seed ATS Pattern

Since 2012: No. 5 seeds are 20-27-1 ATS in first-round games vs. No. 12 seeds
Straight-up record over the same period: 27-21 (5-seeds win more than they lose)
Interpretation: 5-seeds win outright but fail to cover because the spread is inflated by seed perception

Model implication: Remove seed number as a predictive input. Use only efficiency metrics, tempo, and schedule strength (WAB) to generate expected spreads. Compare model output to market line to identify value.

