5. Totals Modeling: Tempo Is the Primary Driver
Blended Tempo Calculation
Expected Possessions = ((Team A AdjTempo + Team B AdjTempo) / 2)
Expected Total = Expected Possessions × ((Team A AdjOE + Team B AdjOE) / 200) × 2

Adjust for neutral site (remove any home-court pace inflation)
When two fast-paced teams meet, interaction compounds upward; two slow teams pull total down significantly

Totals Inefficiency Signals

Public inflates totals for high-profile programs regardless of tempo data
Mid-major totals are set with less precision — books have less data; Torvik has more
eFG% mismatch between two teams' offensive eFG% and the opponent's defensive eFG% allowed signals whether the total is set for a real shooting environment or a reputation one

Model implication: For totals, the most important inputs are: AdjTempo (both teams), AdjOE (both teams), opponent eFG% allowed (both teams). Market totals that deviate more than 4 points from your model projection represent value.

6. Coaching as a Quantifiable Factor

Coaching impact is larger in college basketball than any other major sport, amplified further in single-elimination tournament play
Separate each coach's tournament ATS record from regular-season record — these diverge significantly for some coaches
Coaches who repeatedly cover as favorites demonstrate genuine neutral-site game-planning ability
Available data: Torvik includes coach-level filtering; cross-reference with historical tournament ATS data by coach

Model implication: Add a binary or ordinal coaching feature representing tournament ATS history (e.g., >55% tournament ATS cover rate over last 5 appearances = positive signal).

7. Conference Narrative: What to Ignore
Finding: Conference-level ATS trends are not a reliable predictive input.

The market already prices in conference reputation
Oddsmakers set lines on current-season form, not conference history
Strength of conference varies year to year; the teams, players, and spreads all change
Betting "SEC teams" or fading "Big East teams" based on conference ATS history is a losing strategy

Model implication: Do not include conference as a predictive feature. Use only team-level Torvik metrics.

8. Line Movement as a Signal

First 24 hours after bracket release are highest-value for identifying sharp vs. public money
If a line moves against the seed (e.g., 5-seed opens -7, drops to -5.5), sharp money has acted
Reverse line movement (line moves opposite to betting percentages) is a reliable sharp signal
Mid-major lines are set with less precision and move more in response to sharp action

Model implication: If integrating line movement data, treat reverse line movement as a positive feature for the team receiving sharp action.

9. First-Round Decision Checklist (Apply at Bracket Release)

Pull AdjOE, AdjDE, AdjTempo, eFG%, Barthag, and WAB for every first-round matchup
Compute efficiency differential and expected spread; compare to market opening line
Flag any high seed with AdjDE rank outside the top 60 — potential upset candidate regardless of seed
Compute blended tempo and expected total; compare to market total
Track line movement for 24 hours post-release; note reverse line movement
Apply first-round Under lean to games featuring two top-40 defensive teams
Note Elite Eight region structure — identify which bracket path creates a heavy 2/3/4 chalk situation


